    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Boundary element numerical methods (BEM’s) are well-suited for many problems in solid mechanics which cannot be solved analytically. The interior and exterior boundaries of the region of interest are subdivided into a series of simply-shaped elements across which there is a discontinuity in force or displacement. By building upon the analytical solution for a single element, the stresses and displacements in the body due to all elements is found. A stress or displacement imposed across one element affects the stresses and displacement across all of the others, and in order to match the desired boundary condition on each element one must solve a system of linear equations.\r\n\r\nHere I present a C language computer program called Frac2D which combines a displacement discontinuity BEM with the maximum circumferential stress criterion for fracture (Erdogan and Sih, 1963; Ingraffea, 1981) to calculate fracture paths. Each iteration consists of three steps (figure 1): 1) solving the stresses and displacements at the midpoint of every element using the BEM2;) calculating stress intensity factors for each fracture tip and applying the maximum circumferential stress criterion to see if and in what direction the fracture should propagate; and 3) adding new elements onto the propagating fracture tips to simulate fracture growth. The program assumes a homogeneous, linear elastic, isotropic solid under conditions of two-dimensional plane strain. The BEM is adapted from TWODDa, FORTRAcNode given in Crouch and Starfield (1983). A program similar Frac2D is described by Olson and Pollard (1989) and Olson (1991), and while Frac2D is conceptually little different from their program,its iterative procedure is much more efficient.",
